vitrine is dual-licensed, because it is two things in one repository: a
static-site generator with a provenance gate (software), and a curated,
cited corpus with its accompanying prose (content).

    Software  — MIT License                    (SPDX: MIT)
    Content   — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
                                               (SPDX: CC-BY-SA-4.0)

WHICH LICENSE APPLIES TO WHAT

    Software (MIT):
        src/, tests/, scripts/, k8s/, .github/, and the packaging files
        (pyproject.toml, uv.lock).

    Content (CC BY-SA 4.0):
        data/ (the fact and source registries), docs/, plans/, README.md,
        AGENTS.md, and the rendered site those inputs produce.

Where a file could plausibly be read as either, the content license applies:
the corpus and its methodology are the part of this project meant to be
cited, contested, and built upon, and ShareAlike keeps derivative corpora
open in turn.

